A||||8||Homicidal||Stereo question: Fading the Subwoofer channel...........||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||09-14-2001||06:32 PM||||On my setup, if I fade the Headunit to my front speakers, my Sub channel dissapears, and I hear nothing from it.  If I do the opposite and fade to the rear speakers (headrest), then I get pounding, powerful bass from my sub.  The fine medium between the two is to fade 0 on both, but I find if I fade to the rear speakers just slightly, the bass is much better.  But of course, sound quality is reduced when it's diverted from my 4X10's.<P>My question is, is there any solution to this problem besides getting another headunit with multiple pre-outs?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||09-14-2001||06:48 PM||||as best i can understand you're taking the sub signal off the rear speaker out. why not just take it off the front? or am i missing something?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||09-14-2001||07:00 PM||||Well, my headunit only has one RCA pre-out for an amp, so I have no other choice.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||09-14-2001||07:28 PM||||Swap your front and rear RCA inputs around.  Your fader will be reversed, but at least you'll be able to control the front w/bass & rear balance properly.||||reg||10
Z||000004||Homicidal||09-14-2001||07:57 PM||||Are you speaking on the amplifier?  Or the headunit?<P>The headunit only has one set of RCA outputs, and the sub has one set of RCA inputs.  I tried switching both, but no luck.  I guess I'll try again...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||09-14-2001||09:13 PM||||ok. sounds like the rca's fade with the rear speaker outs. so what fierosound says should work (assuming the head dosen't have any rear signal processing for surround or something). use the rear speaker outs for the front, and the fronts for the rear. then it should work.<P>similarly, if you have a 3 or 4 channel amp you can run the front speakers and sub off the rca's (are the rcas a sub out or full range?) and hook the rears to the front speaker outs (the rears won't need as much anyway). ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||09-15-2001||06:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Homicidal:<BR><B>On my setup, if I fade the Headunit to my front speakers, my Sub channel dissapears, and I hear nothing from it.  If I do the opposite and fade to the rear speakers (headrest), then I get pounding, powerful bass from my sub.  The fine medium between the two is to fade 0 on both, but I find if I fade to the rear speakers just slightly, the bass is much better.  But of course, sound quality is reduced when it's diverted from my 4X10's.<P>My question is, is there any solution to this problem besides getting another headunit with multiple pre-outs?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the same problem, seems I need to wire the front speakers to the rear output and the rear speakers to the front output...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||gargoyle||09-15-2001||05:24 PM||||I don't know how involved you want to get but here is a different idea. Come out of your head unit and split the signal with an rca y adapter. Run one side to your amp as you have it set up. Now run the other side to a separate amp just for the sub. Inline with this install a volume control for the sub(Alpine makes one). This will allow you to control the sub separately. Since you are going to just the sub it shouldn't be affected by fading. Also since the sub draws the most power this should improve your overall sound too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000008||Homicidal||09-15-2001||08:30 PM||||I do not have my speakers amplified, the amp is only for the sub.||||reg||1
